\put\transclude/numbered{false}

\subtree{
  \title{Markdown processing and layout in Swarm}

  \p{As part of my work on [adding a help system to Swarm](https://github.com/swarm-game/swarm/issues/2504), I realized that I needed to better understand our code for processing Markdown (used for in-game item descriptions, scenario descriptions, and the like).  Of course, what started out as innocently reading through the code and adding some extra comments ended with me rewriting a bunch of code from scratch, which I ended up working on obsessively all week.  However, I'm quite pleased with [the result](https://github.com/swarm-game/swarm/pull/2744), which not only fixes several issues we had with spacing and code layout, but also paves the way for easily adding features like [hyphenation](https://github.com/swarm-game/swarm/issues/2746) and [syntax highlighting](https://github.com/swarm-game/swarm/issues/574), in addition to expanding the range of Markdown we support to include links and lists.}
}

\subtree{
  \title{Hanabi}

  \p{[Hanabi](https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Hanabi_(card_game)) is a fascinating cooperative game of logical deduction (if you don't know it I recommend checking it out).  My family has had it for a while, and played it occasionally, but within the past week we have been getting into it a bit more, occasioned by my 8-year-old requesting to be taught how to play.  I played a game with him, and he and my older son have both played games with my wife.  I re-read the [introductory conventions from the H-group](https://hanabi.github.io/), and my older son and I tried playing an online game using some of those conventions. Hopefully soon we can play some larger games with more of us all together (2-player games are a bit odd).}
}

\subtree{
  \title{Piano corner}

  \p{I continued working on [Chopin's waltzes](https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Waltzes_(Chopin)), spending the majority of my time learning [Number 8 in A♭ major](00H6) this week.  It still needs a bit more polishing, but it's largely done.  I also continued polishing a few others, notably No. 12 in F minor/A♭ major.}

  \p{In putting together these weeknotes, I've also realized that what exactly counts as a Chopin waltz is somewhat up for debate! There are some pieces that have been attributed to him but probably aren't authentic; some pieces that are definitely by Chopin but may or may not count as a waltz; some authentic waltzes that we know existed but whose manuscripts have been lost; and some authentic waltzes that were published after his death that he may or may not have wanted to be published.  The Dover edition I have contains 17 waltzes (of which I currently know 14).  After consulting [the Wikipedia page](https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Waltzes_(Chopin)), it seems like there are two more that I want to include in my project: both posthumous waltzes in A minor, the first published in 1955 and the second discovered two years ago at the Morgan Library (which I already learned shortly after it was discovered).}

  \p{I really ought to start creating pages in this forest for pieces in my repertoire...}

  \p{Only a week and a half left until we leave for our yearly month-long pilgrimage to Maine, where I do not have access to a piano.  I was really hoping to finish learning all the waltzes before we leave, but it's looking unlikely that I will have time (I still have approximately four more of them to learn).  Oh well, I can finish the project when we get back.}
}
